From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: QED64 Toolchain <qed64-toolchain@localhost>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 15:42:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/17] fix: select LEAN_SCALAR_PTR_LITERAL layout by pointer
 width, not by Emscripten
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The macro emitted the two-slot 32-bit form whenever LEAN_EMSCRIPTEN was
defined, assuming Emscripten implies ILP32. Under -m64 (wasm64) that
silently corrupts statically-emitted tagged scalars (e.g. Name hashes) by
splitting a 64-bit literal across two 32-bit slots. Gate on
UINTPTR_MAX == UINT32_MAX instead.

Upstreaming: UPSTREAMABLE AS-IS to leanprover/lean4 — the current guard is
wrong the moment any 64-bit Emscripten target exists, and the width-based
guard is correct for both.
---
 src/include/lean/lean.h | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/include/lean/lean.h b/src/include/lean/lean.h
index 23a2105..24a99dc 100644
--- a/src/include/lean/lean.h
+++ b/src/include/lean/lean.h
@@ -3267,7 +3267,7 @@ static inline lean_obj_res lean_runtime_hold(b_lean_obj_arg a) {
 // a native 32-bit build (e.g. the stage0 of the wasm cross-build) must take the
 // two-slot form, or the 64-bit cast truncates the value and leaves the second
 // slot zero, silently corrupting statically-emitted scalars such as Name hashes.
-#if defined(LEAN_EMSCRIPTEN) || UINTPTR_MAX == UINT32_MAX
+#if UINTPTR_MAX == UINT32_MAX
 #define LEAN_SCALAR_PTR_LITERAL(b1, b2, b3, b4, b5, b6, b7, b8) (lean_object*)((uint32_t)b1 | ((uint32_t)b2 << 8) | ((uint32_t)b3 << 16) | ((uint32_t)b4 << 24)), (lean_object*)((uint32_t)b5 | ((uint32_t)b6 << 8) | ((uint32_t)b7 << 16) | ((uint32_t)b8 << 24))
 #else
 #define LEAN_SCALAR_PTR_LITERAL(b1, b2, b3, b4, b5, b6, b7, b8) (lean_object*)((uint64_t)b1 | ((uint64_t)b2 << 8) | ((uint64_t)b3 << 16) | ((uint64_t)b4 << 24) | ((uint64_t)b5 << 32) | ((uint64_t)b6 << 40) | ((uint64_t)b7 << 48) | ((uint64_t)b8 << 56))
